From 1424ccf6becb87698bb42dbb69928cc5b3da1324 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <chris@owen.org>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 04:39:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(v1): pack NewRequestData token IDs as int32 PickleBuffer
 and stage arrays directly

Upstream base: 19f2c20ed4d6ca2efe9bc9f3da8a6c0efc2effc9

Rationale: On request admission, Scheduler.schedule() constructs NewRequestData with both prompt_token_ids and prefill_token_ids (req._all_token_ids, initialized as prompt_token_ids.copy()). Pickling SchedulerOutput across MessageQueue therefore serializes two distinct Python list[int] objects element-by-element (~10 bytes/token total) and allocates 2 * N PyLongObjects on every TP worker process, followed by another O(N) Python list-to-int32 conversion in StagedWriteTensor.stage_write/apply_write and model_state.add_request. Deduplicating fresh prefill_token_ids against prompt_token_ids, pre-packing long prompts (>= 256 tokens) into a 1D int32 array wrapped in Int32TokenIds (serialized via protocol-5 PickleBuffer with a single prefix-length sentinel for prompt_token_ids), and staging ndarray views directly in StagedWriteTensor eliminates per-token Python object serialization and conversion across all ranks.

Quality implications: None (bit-identical int32 token IDs; falls back to list[int] for < 256 tokens or out-of-int32-range values).

Tests: pytest tests/v1/core/test_new_request_data_ipc.py

Upstream status: Local spark-ds41f patch
